    {
      "id": "0385141a-d5a1-4b09-a5c4-3f2ba6b934e0",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Findings\n\nAcross **43 documented Premier League managerial changes (2015/16 → 2024/25)**,\nusing every match result in `matches_full.csv`:\n\n### 1. Do teams earn more points per match after a change?\n\n**Yes on average, but it's a coin flip.** Mean points/game rises from\n**0.93 → 1.04** over the ten matches either side of the change — but the\n**median change is exactly 0.00**, and only **21 of 43 events (49%)**\nimproved. The average is carried by famous bounces: Solskjær at United\n(1.60 → 2.60), Ferguson at Everton (0.70 → 1.90), Rangnick at United\n(1.30 → 2.10). For every bounce there is a change that changed nothing —\nor made things worse (Pulis at West Brom 1.40 → 1.00, Lampard at Chelsea\n1.30 → 0.60).\n\n### 2. Are change teams more likely to avoid relegation?\n\nRaw survival looks bad — **29/43 (67%)** vs the **85%** baseline for all\nEPL team-seasons — but that's selection bias: these teams were already\nstruggling (mean 0.93 ppg before the axe fell). Stratified by how bad\nthey were *at the moment of the change*:\n\n| Position at change | Events | Survived |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bottom half (pos ≥ 11) | 35 | 21 (60%) |\n| Relegation zone (pos ≥ 17) | 21 | 8 (38%) |\n| Bottom two (pos ≥ 19) | 13 | 5 (38%) |\n| Bottom (pos 20) | 7 | 2 (29%) |\n\nEven teams sitting **bottom** at the time of the change survived 2 times\nout of 7. The new-manager bounce is real but modest — it almost never\nrescues a team that was already effectively doomed.\n\n### 3. Does timing matter?\n\n| Change at | n | ppg before → after | Survived |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Early (matchdays 1–10) | 10 | 0.73 → 0.93 | 8/10 (80%) |\n| Middle (md 11–25) | 17 | 0.95 → 1.18 | 10/17 (59%) |\n| Late (md 26+) | 16 | 1.04 → 0.97 | 11/16 (69%) |\n\n**Early changes work best** — they happen to the worst teams (0.73 ppg)\nand have 25+ games to repair the damage, producing the biggest ppg gain\nand highest survival. **Middle changes produce the largest raw ppg jump**\n(0.95 → 1.18, the classic bounce window) yet the *lowest* survival (59%)\n— teams good enough to still be in the mix but bad enough to get a new\nboss are the most precarious. **Late changes are nearly pointless**:\npoints/game actually *falls* slightly (1.04 → 0.97) and there are too\nfew games left for a bounce to matter — yet boards keep pulling the\ntrigger in April anyway.\n\n**Bottom line:** the bounce is real but oversold. On average a new\nmanager adds ~+0.1 ppg — worth roughly 4 points over a season — and the\nsurvival benefit is concentrated in early-season changes. Sacking a\nmanager in March is closer to a statement than a strategy.",
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    }
  ]
